-- allium: 3
-- spacetime-snapshot.allium
--
-- Scope: The mechanics underneath spacetime-seed.allium's snapshot, restore
--        and import: what the artefact's values look like so that two dumps of the
--        same board agree whichever store produced them, how rows cross the
--        boundary into and out of the shared store, and WHICH store each
--        operator command reads from or writes to — the managed one, a named
--        one, or the `spacetime` CLI's own default.
-- Includes: the store-neutral value representation of a snapshot (canonical
--           booleans, absence as null, integers as integers, byte vectors as
--           number arrays, rows in id order); the sentinel translation at the
--           store boundary; the size bound on one write to the store and the
--           per-row column backstop; the store each snapshot command targets
--           and whether it brings the managed store up; where the managed
--           store's data, module-hash record and log live; and that a board
--           process stops its managed store at most once.
-- Retired: the local (SQLite) dump and its per-table extract sources
--          (DumpLocalBoard, ExtractSource, LocalTableSource and the rules that
--          read each table), and the seed's store selection — removed with
--          `dispatch spacetime dump` and `dispatch spacetime seed` in task
--          #16755 (spacetime-seed.allium: "Seeding (removed)"). Every snapshot
--          is now a dump of a store.
-- Excludes: what a snapshot must contain, the restore's refusals, the
--           id-sequence burn, idempotent restore and the import's add-only
--           semantics (all spacetime-seed.allium — not
--           restated here); the managed store's lifecycle on a board launch —
--           select, adopt, start, publish, connect, stop on exit and on abort
--           (startup.allium: ManagedStoreHost and the rules from
--           AbortWhenTheManagedStoreHasNoCli to
--           StopTheManagedStoreWhenStartupAborts, plus
--           ImportBringsUpTheManagedStore); the subscription and first
--           connection (sync.allium); and the in-process
--           reducer stand-in (spacetime-memory-store.allium), which is
--           unrelated to the snapshot's own in-memory test store.
--
-- Why this exists beside spacetime-seed.allium. That file says what a
-- snapshot is FOR and what restoring one must preserve; it deliberately says
-- nothing about transport or encoding. But two of its promises are only kept
-- by decisions taken at exactly that level. "A dump of the same board is the
-- same file whichever store dumped it" is what lets a diff between two
-- backups be read at all, and it depends on value
-- representation. "Rows travel whole" depends on a store boundary that
-- translates the store's sentinels back to absence rather than leaking them.
-- And "which store does this command touch" is the question an operator in
-- an incident most needs answered, with the managed store now a store that
-- appears and disappears with the board.

use "./spacetime-seed.allium" as seed

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Enumerations
------------------------------------------------------------

-- The value a store-side column holds to mean "absent", for columns the store
-- keeps non-optional although the domain treats them as absent-able.
enum Sentinel {
    empty_string -- paths, timestamps, urls, tags, identities, labels
    | zero       -- id references, and epics.feed_interval_secs
}

-- The operator commands that read or write a whole store's shared tables, and
-- the board itself for contrast.
enum StoreCommand {
    board          -- `dispatch tui`
    | dump_server  -- `dispatch spacetime dump-server`
    | restore      -- `dispatch spacetime restore`
    | import_store -- `dispatch store import`
}

-- What a command ends up talking to.
enum StoreTarget {
    named_store          -- the store named by --spacetime-server or DISPATCH_SPACETIME_SERVER
    | named_by_flag      -- the store named by the command's own --server flag
    | managed_store      -- the managed store, brought up for the command and stopped after it
    | cli_default_server -- whatever server the operator's `spacetime` CLI is configured for
}

-- One column the store keeps non-optional with a sentinel standing for
-- absence. The full list is src/spacetime/snapshot.rs's sentinel_columns.
entity SentinelColumn {
    table: seed/SharedTable
    column: String
    sentinel: Sentinel
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Entities
------------------------------------------------------------

-- Which store one invocation of a command resolved to.
entity StoreSelection {
    command: StoreCommand
    named_store: String?   -- --spacetime-server, else DISPATCH_SPACETIME_SERVER; blank counts as absent
    server_flag: String?   -- the command's own --server, where it has one
    target: StoreTarget

    -- Bringing the managed store up is what makes a command responsible for
    -- stopping it. Only two commands ever do (see the selection rules).
    brings_up_managed_store: target = managed_store
}

-- The managed store as it sits on disk. Its lifecycle is startup.allium's;
-- what is pinned here is WHERE it lives, which decides whether two launches
-- find the same data.
entity ManagedStoreLocation {
    data_dir: String       -- <dispatch's default data directory>/spacetime, whatever --db says
    module_hash_record: String -- a file in data_dir: startup.allium's recorded_module_hash
    log_file: String       -- beside the launch's own app.log, i.e. under --db's directory
}

-- One board (or import) process's hold on the managed store.
entity ManagedStoreHold {
    held: Boolean              -- startup.allium: managed_store_held_by_this_board()
    stop_requested: Boolean    -- a stop has already been asked for by this process
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Config
------------------------------------------------------------

config {
    -- The directory, under dispatch's DEFAULT data directory, that the
    -- managed store serves. Not under --db's directory: see
    -- ManagedStoreDataIgnoresTheBoardDatabase.
    managed_store_data_dir_name: String = "spacetime"

    -- The module-hash record startup.allium's ConnectWhenTheManagedModuleIsCurrent
    -- compares against, inside the managed store's data directory.
    module_hash_file_name: String = "managed-module.sha256"

    -- The managed store's own stdout and stderr, beside app.log.
    managed_store_log_name: String = "managed-store.log"

    -- The database every snapshot command defaults to on a server.
    default_database_name: String = "dispatch"

    -- The largest encoded batch of rows sent to the store in one write. The
    -- bound is in bytes because the transport's limit is in bytes: one write
    -- is one command-line argument, capped by the kernel per argument, and
    -- rows vary by two orders of magnitude in size. Mirrors BYTES_PER_CALL in
    -- src/spacetime/cli_store.rs.
    max_bytes_per_store_write: Integer = 98304
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Rules: the store boundary
------------------------------------------------------------

rule DumpAStore {
    when: DumpStore(operator, server, database)

    ensures:
        StoreSnapshotTaken(
            snapshot: seed/Snapshot.created(
                format_version: seed/config.snapshot_format_version,
                taken_at: now,
                extracts: read_each_store_table(server, database)
            )
        )

    @guidance
        -- The store's own dump: every shared table's columns as the store's
        -- published schema names them, and its rows decoded to plain column
        -- name and value, ordered by id client-side (the store's query
        -- language refuses an ordering clause). Sentinels are translated back
        -- to absence on the way out (SentinelsNeverReachTheSnapshot).
        --
        -- A wire shape this side does not recognise is an error, never a
        -- defaulted value: a backup that silently decodes a column as absent
        -- is worse than one that will not be taken.
        --
        -- SEE THE OPEN QUESTION on one-moment reads: each table is read by a
        -- separate query, which seed/TakeSnapshot's "reads one moment, not
        -- ten" does not allow.
}

rule WriteRowsToTheStore {
    when: WriteRows(store, table, rows)

    ensures:
        for row in rows:
            RowWritten(table: table, row: with_sentinels_for_absence(table, row))

    @guidance
        -- The write half of the boundary, used by seed/RestoreSnapshot and
        -- seed/ImportOldStore alike. Absence in the
        -- snapshot becomes the column's sentinel; a value the store's schema
        -- marks optional is wrapped as present. Which columns are optional is
        -- asked of the store's published schema, not hard-coded: wrapping a
        -- column that is not optional fails as loudly as not wrapping one that
        -- is, and the only authority on which is which is the module actually
        -- running.
        --
        -- Rows are sent in batches no larger than
        -- config.max_bytes_per_store_write, measured on the encoded bytes
        -- that are actually sent. Many batches means many store transactions,
        -- which is why seed/RestoreSnapshot is resumable rather than atomic.
}

rule RefuseARowTooLargeForOneWrite {
    when: WriteRows(store, table, rows)
    requires: rows.any(r => encoded_size(table, r) > config.max_bytes_per_store_write)

    ensures: StoreWriteFailed(table: table, detail: oversized_row_detail(table, rows))

    @guidance
        -- A single row that cannot fit in one write is named — its table, its
        -- size and its id — rather than sent and rejected by the operating
        -- system with a message that names neither. The snapshot is fine; it
        -- is this transport that cannot carry the row.
        --
        -- This is a store FAILURE, not one of seed/RefusalReason's refusals,
        -- and it is discovered while writing. See the open question on
        -- whether it should be checked before the burn.
}

rule RefuseAColumnTheStoreDoesNotHave {
    when: WriteRows(store, table, rows)
    requires: rows.any(r => carries_column_not_in_store(table, r) or lacks_column_in_store(table, r))

    ensures: StoreWriteFailed(table: table, detail: column_drift_detail(table, rows))

    @guidance
        -- A BACKSTOP, checked per row in both directions. The gate is
        -- seed/RefuseMismatchedSchema, which compares every extract's declared
        -- columns before anything is written
        -- (seed/NothingIsWrittenBeforeTheChecksPass). This catches a row whose
        -- own keys disagree with its extract's declared columns — a
        -- hand-edited file, say. The dangerous direction is a column the row
        -- carries and the store lacks: without this it would simply not be
        -- copied, and the write would report success having dropped it.
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Rules: which store each command targets
------------------------------------------------------------

rule DumpServerAndRestoreUseTheirOwnServerFlag {
    when: SelectStore(selection)
    requires: selection.command = dump_server or selection.command = restore

    ensures:
        if selection.server_flag != null:
            selection.target = named_by_flag
        else:
            selection.target = cli_default_server

    @guidance
        -- Both take --server and --database (default
        -- config.default_database_name). With no --server the `spacetime`
        -- CLI's own configured default server is used, which is what an
        -- operator who has run `spacetime login` expects. Neither consults
        -- --spacetime-server or DISPATCH_SPACETIME_SERVER, and neither starts,
        -- publishes to or stops the managed store: restoring into the managed
        -- store means it must already be running and be what the CLI default
        -- or --server points at. See the open question.
}

rule ImportTargetsTheNamedStoreOrBringsUpTheManagedOne {
    when: SelectStore(selection)
    requires: selection.command = import_store

    ensures:
        if selection.named_store != null:
            selection.target = named_store
        else:
            selection.target = managed_store

    @guidance
        -- Stated here only so that the commands can be read side by side;
        -- the behaviour is startup.allium's ImportBringsUpTheManagedStore. A
        -- missing `spacetime` CLI is refused before anything is started, with
        -- an install hint, as for a board launch.
}

rule BoardTargetsTheNamedStoreOrBringsUpTheManagedOne {
    when: SelectStore(selection)
    requires: selection.command = board

    ensures:
        if selection.named_store != null:
            selection.target = named_store
        else:
            selection.target = managed_store

    @guidance
        -- startup.allium: BringUpTheManagedStoreOnceTheHostIsNamed and
        -- ConnectToTheNamedStoreOnceTheHostIsNamed. Repeated only for the
        -- comparison above.
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Rules: the managed store's location and stop
------------------------------------------------------------

rule ManagedStoreDataIgnoresTheBoardDatabase {
    when: ManagedStoreLocated(location, board_database)

    ensures: location.data_dir = default_data_dir() + "/" + config.managed_store_data_dir_name
    ensures: location.module_hash_record = location.data_dir + "/" + config.module_hash_file_name
    ensures: location.log_file = directory_of(board_database) + "/" + config.managed_store_log_name

    @guidance
        -- `board_database` is the path --db names; only its directory is
        -- used (storage.allium: DataDirectory) — no database is opened.
        --
        -- THE DATA FOLLOWS THE MACHINE, THE LOG FOLLOWS THE LAUNCH. A
        -- throwaway --db (a worktree build, a scratch run) must not start a
        -- second managed store over an empty data directory, nor lose sight of
        -- the module hash the real one recorded — so the data directory, and
        -- the hash record inside it, are fixed under dispatch's default data
        -- directory regardless of --db. The store's own log goes beside the
        -- launch's app.log, where the operator reading that launch's log will
        -- look. This is also why a scratch `dispatch tui --db /tmp/x.db`
        -- with no store named adopts — and on exit stops — the operator's
        -- real managed store.
        --
        -- The module that gets published is written out into the same data
        -- directory first, so a publish never depends on the binary's own
        -- location.
}

rule StopTheManagedStoreAtMostOnce {
    when: ManagedStoreStopRequested(hold)
    requires: hold.held
    requires: not hold.stop_requested

    ensures: hold.stop_requested = true
    ensures: StopSignalSent(to: processes_serving_the_managed_address())

    @guidance
        -- startup.allium decides WHEN a held store is stopped; this pins that
        -- a process stops it at most once. An abort inside bring-up followed
        -- by the exit path, or a termination signal racing a quit, would
        -- otherwise ask twice, and the second ask could land on a store a
        -- NEW board has started on the same address in between.
        --
        -- The stop is a termination signal to every process listening on the
        -- managed address and to the store this process spawned, then a wait
        -- bounded by startup's config.managed_store_stop_timeout for all of
        -- them to be gone. Listening processes are found by asking the
        -- operating system who holds the port, because an adopted store was
        -- not this process's child.
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Invariants
------------------------------------------------------------

-- A process only ever asks to stop a store it holds.
invariant StopOnlyWhatIsHeld {
    for h in ManagedStoreHolds:
        h.stop_requested implies h.held
}

-- sort_order is never given a sentinel: zero is a real sort order there, and
-- absence means "fall back to the id" (see SentinelsNeverReachTheSnapshot).
invariant SortOrderHasNoSentinel {
    for c in SentinelColumns:
        c.column != "sort_order"
}

-- SnapshotValuesAreStoreNeutral: a snapshot's values do not depend on which
-- store produced it. A boolean is true/false, never 0/1. An integer stays an integer —
-- an id that round-trips as 4096.0 compares unequal to 4096 while looking
-- right in a diff. A byte vector is an array of byte values, the shape the
-- store's own query output uses. Absence is null, never a sentinel. Rows are
-- in id order and the file is pretty-printed, so two backups diff line by
-- line. A value with no faithful representation — a non-finite float, a
-- boolean column holding anything but 0, 1 or null — is refused, never
-- written under a different name. Consequence: a dump of a store and a dump
-- of another store it was restored into are the same file, modulo taken_at. Not expressible as a state assertion: it relates the outputs of
-- two different readers.

-- SentinelsNeverReachTheSnapshot: a SentinelColumn's sentinel exists only
-- inside the store. The write boundary turns absence into it and the read
-- boundary turns it back into absence, and nothing else in the system
-- consumes the sentinel list but those two ends of one conversion (plus the
-- schema parity test that reads it). A reader that skipped the mapping would
-- see epic_id 0 as "belongs to epic 0" and owner "" as "owned by the empty
-- person"; both look plausible and are wrong.
--
-- WHY SENTINELS EXIST AT ALL. The store's query language cannot filter on an
-- optional column — there is no syntax for it — and a subscription is a
-- filter (sync.allium: SendsOnlyWhatWasSubscribedTo). So every absent-able
-- column anybody might subscribe by is stored non-optional, with a value
-- unreachable as a real one standing for absence: zero for id references
-- (the store's own "no id supplied"; real ids start at 1) and for
-- epics.feed_interval_secs (whose minimum is well above zero), the empty
-- string for everything else (host labels lean on host.allium: RenameHost
-- refusing an empty label). Changing a column's type later is not
-- automigratable, which is why the list covers more columns than are filtered
-- on today. sort_order is deliberately excluded: zero is a real sort order and
-- absence means "fall back to the id", so a sentinel would reorder cards.

-- AStoreIsOnlyStartedForTheCommandsThatStopIt: of the commands above, only
-- the board and an import with no store named ever start or adopt the managed
-- store, and both stop it on every exit they can run (startup.allium:
-- TheManagedStoreStopsWithItsBoard, ImportBringsUpTheManagedStore). A command
-- that reaches the CLI's default server (dump-server, restore) never starts,
-- publishes to or stops anything, so it can never leave a store behind.

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Surfaces
------------------------------------------------------------

surface SnapshotTargets {
    facing operator: seed/Operator

    exposes:
        for selection in StoreSelections:
            selection.command
            selection.target
            selection.brings_up_managed_store
        for location in ManagedStoreLocations:
            location.data_dir
            location.module_hash_record
            location.log_file

    provides:
        DumpStore(operator, server, database)
        SelectStore(selection)

    @guarantee EveryCommandSaysWhichStoreItTouched
        -- Each command reports the server it wrote to or read from: a
        -- restore its row and table counts, an import its per-table imported / already-there / dropped counts.
        -- During an incident "which store did that just change" must not
        -- need to be inferred from flags and environment.

    @guidance
        -- The restore and import commands are spacetime-seed.allium's
        -- SnapshotCommandLine; this surface adds only the store each resolves
        -- to. There is still deliberately no MCP twin for any of them.
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Open Questions
------------------------------------------------------------

open question "A dump of a STORE (`dispatch spacetime dump-server`, and the import's read of its source) reads each shared table with a separate query, so a write landing between two reads can produce a snapshot holding a watch whose task is absent. spacetime-seed.allium's TakeSnapshot requires one consistent read ('reads one moment, not ten'). Is the store dump an accepted exception (a store dump is usually taken from a quiet store), or must it read one moment — and if so, through what, since the CLI offers no multi-statement snapshot read?"

open question "`dispatch spacetime restore` and `dump-server` default to the `spacetime` CLI's own configured server and ignore --spacetime-server / DISPATCH_SPACETIME_SERVER, while `store import` honours them and falls back to the managed store. Is that split intended (restore and dump-server are pointed at a server deliberately, every time), or should all three resolve the store the same way — and should restore into the managed store bring it up as import does?"

open question "A row too large for one store write is discovered while writing — after the id sequences have been burned and earlier tables written — so it fails a restore partway rather than refusing it up front, which is the shape seed/NothingIsWrittenBeforeTheChecksPass exists to rule out. The restore is resumable, but re-running it fails on the same row. Should the size check join the up-front refusals?"
